(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-cytopenias-hemochromatosis-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cytopenias and Hemochromatosis Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has fatigue and dark urine. Hemoglobin is 8.8 g/dL (normal 12.0-16.0 g/dL), reticulocytes 6% (normal 0.5-2.5%), and her peripheral smear shows many schistocytes (red blood cell fragments). Which process is most likely?","choices":["Sideroblastic anemia","Aplastic anemia","Hemolysis","Megaloblastic anemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Schistocytes, red blood cell fragments, are the hallmark finding of hemolysis on the peripheral smear. Her anemia with a high reticulocyte count and dark urine fits premature destruction of red cells with a compensating marrow. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 61, 68, 70"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with autoimmune hemolytic anemia arrives confused, with heart rate 128/min and blood pressure 82/50 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 5.2 g/dL (normal 12.0-16.0 g/dL), down from 7.9 g/dL 2 days ago. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stabilize; call the blood bank","Begin outpatient steroids","Schedule elective splenectomy","Recheck counts in 1 week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immediate stabilization is considered when hemoglobin is below 6 g/dL, falls rapidly or the patient is unstable, and she has all three. Contact the blood bank early. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with known glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency has 2 days of dysuria and urinary frequency, and his urinalysis confirms a urinary tract infection. Which of these medications should be avoided?","choices":["Oral hydration","Amoxicillin","Nitrofurantoin","Cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitrofurantoin is on the list of common triggers of acute hemolytic anemia in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, quinolones and phenazopyridine. His urinary tract infection needs an agent that is not an oxidant trigger. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation takes apixaban. He has fatigue and dark stools; hemoglobin is 6.9 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 68 fL (normal 80-100 fL) and ferritin 4 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL). Besides a gastrointestinal consult, what should be done about his anticoagulant?","choices":["Stop it after consulting cardiology","Continue it and start oral iron alone","Add aspirin for stroke protection","Continue it with erythropoietin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His microcytic anemia with a very low ferritin is severe iron deficiency, likely from gastrointestinal blood loss. Anticoagulants are discontinued when the anemia is severe, in consultation with cardiology, because his atrial fibrillation is the reason he takes one. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man recovering from a viral illness has a white blood cell count of 3,000 cells per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). His differential shows neutrophils 35% (normal 40-70%), bands 5% (normal 0-5%), lymphocytes 52% and monocytes 8%. What is his absolute neutrophil count?","choices":["150 cells per microliter","1,050 cells per microliter","1,200 cells per microliter","1,560 cells per microliter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absolute neutrophil count = white count x (neutrophils + bands)% / 100 = 3,000 x (35 + 5) / 100 = 1,200 cells per microliter, which is mild neutropenia (1,000-1,500). Transient viral leukopenia often resolves after recovery. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has a hemoglobin of 11.0 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 78 fL (normal 80-100 fL), ferritin 140 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL) and total iron-binding capacity 260 mcg/dL (normal 250-450 mcg/dL). He has no known infection or autoimmune disease. Which test helps decide whether inflammation is affecting his ferritin?","choices":["Fecal occult blood test and urinalysis","Bone marrow aspirate for ring sideroblasts","Repeat ferritin after a month of iron","Sedimentation rate or C-reactive protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ferritin is an acute phase reactant, so an inflammatory condition can lift it into the normal range even with iron deficiency; a normal ferritin does not exclude iron deficiency. When inflammation is not clinically known, an erythrocyte sedimentation rate and/or C-reactive protein can help. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 14, 25"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman has confirmed B12 deficiency with a megaloblastic anemia. Pernicious anemia is suspected. Which blood test is specific for pernicious anemia, though not sensitive?","choices":["Homocysteine level","Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies","Anti-parietal cell antibodies","Methylmalonic acid level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune reaction against parietal cells and/or intrinsic factor. Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies can sometimes be detected in the blood and are specific but not sensitive, so a positive result supports the diagnosis while a negative one does not exclude it. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 44, 47, 48, 50"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl with sickle cell disease is seen for a well-child visit. Her parents ask what can prevent serious infections, since children with her disease are susceptible to encapsulated bacteria. Which preventive plan is indicated?","choices":["Penicillin and vaccines","Iron supplements","Postpone vaccines","Hydroxyurea instead of prophylaxis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sickle cell disease brings increased susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria, so prevention is vaccinations, indicated childhood penicillin prophylaxis and infection education. Prevention and emergency care vary with age, genotype and clinical history. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 93, 98, 107"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has pallor and fatigue; his maternal uncle had the same anemia. His hemoglobin is 9.0 g/dL (normal 11.5-15.5 g/dL), and his marrow shows ring sideroblasts (iron-containing mitochondria in a ring around the nucleus). He takes no medications and has no lead exposure. Which type of sideroblastic anemia fits best?","choices":["Acquired reversible, from lead","Hereditary, usually X-linked","Acquired, from myelodysplasia","Acquired reversible, from a drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sideroblastic anemia has three types: hereditary (usually X-linked), acquired (usually myelodysplastic syndrome) and acquired reversible (alcohol, drugs, copper deficiency, hypothermia, lead). About two thirds of cases present in males in early childhood or adolescence; an affected boy with an affected maternal uncle and no exposures fits the hereditary type. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 29, 30"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a microcytic anemia (hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL, normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL; mean corpuscular volume 74 fL, normal 80-100 fL) took oral iron correctly for 3 months with no rise in hemoglobin. Colonoscopy and endoscopy were normal, and her iron studies are equivocal, so the diagnosis is not confirmed. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiology referral","Dietitian referral","Repeat gastroenterology referral","Hematology referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Referral to hematology is recommended when the diagnosis of iron deficiency is not confirmed or the patient is unresponsive to oral iron therapy. She has both: no response after 3 months of correct use and equivocal iron studies. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with advanced chronic kidney disease has a hemoglobin of 8.9 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) despite iron repletion, and his primary care clinician is unsure whether to begin an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Gastroenterology","Oncology","Nephrology","Hematology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nephrology referral is advised for advanced chronic kidney disease, erythropoiesis-stimulating agent decisions or uncertainty, and he has all three. Anemia of chronic kidney disease is often chronic and may improve with iron or an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent as indicated. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 53, 58, 59, 106"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has fatigue. Her hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 71 fL (normal 80-100 fL) and mean corpuscular hemoglobin 22 pg (normal 27-31 pg). How should her anemia be classified by these indices?","choices":["Normocytic and normochromic","Macrocytic and normochromic","Microcytic and hyperchromic","Microcytic and hypochromic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her mean corpuscular volume of 71 fL is below 80 fL, which is microcytosis, and her mean corpuscular hemoglobin of 22 pg is below 27 pg, which is hypochromia (pale cells). That microcytic, hypochromic pattern is the complete blood count of iron deficiency and of its differential. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 7, 15"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with untreated hypothyroidism has fatigue and a hemoglobin of 10.8 g/dL (normal 12-16 g/dL). Her mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (normal 80-100 fL). B12, folate, homocysteine and methylmalonic acid levels are normal, and the smear shows no macro-ovalocytes or hypersegmented neutrophils. How is her anemia best classified?","choices":["Megaloblastic macrocytic","Microcytic","Normocytic","Non-megaloblastic macrocytic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A mean corpuscular volume above 100 fL makes her anemia macrocytic, and normal B12, folate and metabolite levels with no megaloblastic smear changes make it non-megaloblastic. Hypothyroidism is a listed non-megaloblastic cause, and such causes usually keep the volume below 115 fL, as hers is. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 7, 38, 39, 47, 52"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with iron deficiency anemia (hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL, normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) asks how to take her liquid ferrous sulfate so that she absorbs it best with the fewest side effects. Which instruction is most appropriate?","choices":["Empty stomach with orange juice","With a meal and a glass of milk","At bedtime with an antacid tablet","With breakfast and a cup of tea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The better-absorbed oral preparation is taken in the morning on an empty stomach with a full glass of orange juice: its low pH, citric acid and vitamin C help uptake and reduce exposure of free iron to the rest of the gut, limiting side effects. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 18, 19"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman with sickle cell disease has had four vaso-occlusive pain crises requiring admission in the past year. She is stable between crises and wants a treatment that changes the course of her disease. Which drug is indicated?","choices":["Methylene blue","Folic acid","Hydroxyurea","Penicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Disease modification in sickle cell disease is hydroxyurea, which raises fetal hemoglobin and reduces vaso-occlusive events. Her repeated crises are exactly what it is used to reduce; fetal hemoglobin of 10 to 20 percent lessens clinical severity. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with sickle cell disease is admitted for a pain crisis. On day 2 he develops fever, chest pain, shortness of breath and hypoxemia, and his chest radiograph shows a new pulmonary infiltrate. Which complication does this represent?","choices":["Acute chest syndrome","Splenic infarction","Aseptic necrosis of the hip","Uncomplicated pain crisis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute chest syndrome should be considered in sickle cell disease with chest pain, fever, shortness of breath, hypoxemia and pulmonary infiltrates on a chest radiograph. It is often associated with an acute pain event, is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease and carries more than 10 percent mortality in adults. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has 3 weeks of fatigue, two recent bacterial infections, gum bleeding and petechiae. Hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), white blood cell count 1,600 cells per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000), platelets 18,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000) and the reticulocyte count is low. The smear shows no hypersegmented neutrophils, and B12 and folate levels are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Folate deficiency","Aplastic anemia","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Anemia of chronic kidney disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He has pancytopenia, loss of all three cell lines, with recurrent infections from neutropenia, bleeding and petechiae from thrombocytopenia, and fatigue from anemia. Loss of hematopoietic stem cells defines aplastic anemia, and half of cases occur in the first three decades, with a small peak between ages 20 and 25. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 39, 52, 55, 58"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man recovered from an episode of hemolysis triggered by fava beans, and a low glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase level later confirmed the deficiency. Which instruction should be part of his education?","choices":["Fava beans are safe cooked","Seek care for dark urine","Take daily iron tablets","Expect a splenectomy soon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Education for this deficiency is to avoid oxidative triggers and seek care for jaundice or dark urine, the signs of an acute hemolytic attack. With trigger avoidance the prognosis is usually favorable. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 79, 82, 107"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy with severe beta thalassemia has received frequent red cell transfusions for years. He now has fatigue and a rising ferritin. Which complication of his disease and its treatment is most concerning?","choices":["Iron overload","Iron deficiency","Autoimmune hemolysis","Folate excess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Iron overload is part of the clinical course of thalassemia, and transfusion, used only when absolutely necessary, can exacerbate it. His years of transfusions and rising ferritin point to iron loading, which calls for iron monitoring in follow-up. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 87, 89, 107"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has fatigue. Her hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 82 fL (normal 80-100 fL), ferritin 310 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL), total iron-binding capacity 210 mcg/dL (normal 250-450 mcg/dL), transferrin saturation 12% (normal 20-50%) and reticulocyte count 0.4% (normal 0.5-2.5%). Which anemia do these results fit?","choices":["Sideroblastic anemia","Coexistent iron deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency anemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatoid arthritis is an inflammatory condition, and her panel fits anemia of chronic disease: a mild anemia, normal mean corpuscular volume, ferritin normal or increased (310 ng/mL), total iron-binding capacity decreased (210 mcg/dL), low transferrin saturation and a reticulocyte count that is usually low. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 13, 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has fatigue with a hemoglobin of 9.8 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 72 fL (normal 80-100 fL) and ferritin 6 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL). A fecal occult blood test (stool guaiac test) is positive. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Hematology for a bone marrow biopsy and smear","Cardiology for an echocardiogram before iron","Dietitian for a high-iron diet before more tests","Gastroenterology for colonoscopy and endoscopy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His microcytic anemia and low ferritin are iron deficiency, and the positive stool guaiac test points to gastrointestinal blood loss. A gastrointestinal bleed calls for a gastrointestinal consult for colonoscopy and endoscopy to evaluate for colorectal cancer, while the cause is treated and iron replaced. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman who works at an indoor firing range has mild abdominal colic and constipation and no neurologic symptoms. Her blood lead level is mildly elevated and her hemoglobin is 11.6 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL). What is the first step in management?","choices":["Begin oral iron therapy","Stop the lead exposure","Start succimer chelation","Start dimercaprol injections"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of lead poisoning starts with stopping exposure, and further treatment is based on the lead level. Chelating agents are kept for severe cases with neurologic effects, which she does not have. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has fatigue and new tingling in his feet. His mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (normal 80-100 fL) and the smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. His B12 level is 230 pg/mL (normal 200-900 pg/mL), at the borderline, and his folate level is normal. Clinical suspicion of B12 deficiency continues. Which test should be ordered next?","choices":["Methylmalonic acid level","Anti-parietal cell antibodies","Bone marrow biopsy","Ferritin and transferrin saturation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. B12 and folate levels are the initial tests but are not very sensitive, and his B12 is borderline while suspicion continues. Methylmalonic acid rises with B12 deficiency but not with folate deficiency, because its conversion to succinyl CoA requires only B12, so it settles the question. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 47, 50"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man with beta thalassemia major has iron overload from years of transfusions. He continues to need transfusions, so phlebotomy is unsuitable. Which treatment should be considered for the overload?","choices":["More frequent transfusion","Iron chelation","Therapeutic phlebotomy","Glucocorticoids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Secondary iron overload is managed by treating the cause and considering chelation when phlebotomy is unsuitable. A patient who still needs transfusion for anemia cannot have blood removed, so chelation is the option. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 74, 89, 104"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with lead poisoning from refinishing old painted furniture has been removed from the exposure. He has confusion, irritability and peripheral neuropathy, a severely elevated blood lead level, a hemoglobin of 10.9 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) and basophilic stippling (an abnormal collection of nucleic acids in the cells) on his smear. Which treatment is indicated now?","choices":["Oral iron replacement","Oral pyridoxine","Epoetin alfa","Chelation with succimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lead poisoning is treated by stopping exposure and according to the lead level; in severe cases with neurologic effects, chelating agents can be used: succimer, dimercaprol or ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid. He is already removed from the source and has severe neurologic involvement. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has new anemia with jaundice and splenomegaly. Hemoglobin is 9.4 g/dL (normal 12.0-16.0 g/dL) and reticulocytes 7% (normal 0.5-2.5%), and hemolytic anemia is suspected. Which test should be ordered to look for its hallmark finding?","choices":["Bone marrow biopsy","Hemoglobin electrophoresis","Prussian blue liver biopsy","Peripheral blood smear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A peripheral blood smear should be ordered on a patient with suspected hemolytic anemia; the hallmark finding of hemolysis is schistocytes, red blood cell fragments. It belongs to the workup with a complete blood count, reticulocytes, bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin and urinalysis. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 68, 69"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with autoimmune hemolytic anemia confirmed by a positive direct antiglobulin test. He is hemodynamically stable with hemoglobin 8.1 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), and glucocorticoids have been started. Which order should also be part of his admission?","choices":["Thromboembolism prophylaxis","Immediate splenectomy","Oral iron supplement","Emergency transfusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Autoimmune hemolytic anemia is associated with an increased risk of venous thromboembolism, so remember venous thromboembolism prophylaxis for inpatients, and consider it in outpatients. As an inpatient with this disease he should have it ordered. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man has iron deficiency anemia, and evaluation finds no menstrual or obvious source of loss. He asks what happens next while he takes his iron. Which follow-up is appropriate?","choices":["Refer for erythropoietin","Seek the loss; refer if needed","Urgent hematology referral","Monitor neurologic recovery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Follow-up of iron deficiency is to identify blood loss, reinforce iron use and refer to gastrointestinal specialists if indicated. With the cause and iron stores corrected, it usually resolves. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a white blood cell count of 2,400 cells per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). Her differential shows neutrophils 30% (normal 40-70%), bands 5% (normal 0-5%), lymphocytes 55% and monocytes 10%. How is her neutropenia classified?","choices":["Not neutropenic","Mild","Moderate","Severe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absolute neutrophil count = white count x (neutrophils + bands)% / 100 = 2,400 x 35 / 100 = 840 cells per microliter. Moderate neutropenia is 500-999, mild is 1,000-1,500 and severe is below 500. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman has aplastic anemia confirmed by bone marrow biopsy. Her sister is a suitable donor. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent","Folic acid supplementation","Observation with repeat counts","Bone marrow transplant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The treatment of aplastic anemia is bone marrow transplant, and five-year survival is above 75% for patients transplanted from a suitable donor, as her sister is. Most untreated patients die within one year from disease-related complications. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 55, 58, 60"}]};
